-- title:
--   Theorem 4.13 — every hyperfinite purely atomic Bayesian game admits a Harsányi $\varepsilon$-equilibrium for every $\varepsilon>0$
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a purely atomic Bayesian game on a standard Borel state space $\Omega$: a finite non-empty set $I$ of players, finite non-empty action sets $A_i$ (which may differ between players), type functions $t^i$, a prior $\mu^i$ for each player, and bounded Borel payoffs $r^i$. Suppose the common knowledge relation of $B$ is hyperfinite. Then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a strategy profile $\sigma$ (each $\sigma^i$ Borel and constant on player $i$'s knowledge classes) such that for every player $i$
--   $$\int_\Omega\Big[\max_{a\in A_i}r^i(a,\sigma^{-i}\mid t^i_\omega)-r^i(\sigma\mid t^i_\omega)\Big]\,d\mu^i(\omega)\le\varepsilon .$$
--
--   Such games may have no Bayesian (interim) $\varepsilon$-equilibrium, so the theorem locates the divergence between ex ante and interim equilibrium existence at the hyperfinite rung of the CBER hierarchy.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: Ω a Polish space with its Borel σ-algebra → Ω a standard Borel space; reason: every Polish space with its Borel σ-algebra is standard Borel, and every notion in the statement depends only on the measurable structure, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - encoding: a purely atomic Bayesian game (Ω, I, t, (µⁱ), A, r) with knowledge relations Eᵢ → the structure BayesianGame: Eᵢ is tⁱ_x = tⁱ_y, purely atomic is countability of its classes, the prior condition (2.2) is (µⁱ).bind tⁱ = µⁱ, payoffs are given on pure profiles; reason: the paper defines Eᵢ from the types (p.11); the second clause of (2.2) and the purely atomic types of Definition 2.9 follow from the field `knows`
--   - encoding: rⁱ(σ | τⁱ) = Σ_{ω | tⁱ(ω)=τⁱ} rⁱ(ω, σ(ω)) τⁱ(ω) (3.1) → the integral of ω' ↦ rⁱ(ω', σ(ω')) against τⁱ = tⁱ_ω; reason: τⁱ is concentrated on the countable class {ω' | tⁱ(ω') = τⁱ}, so the integral is that sum
--   - encoding: mixed actions in ∆(A) → probability mass functions PMF A; Borel strategies are those with every coordinate g ↦ σ(g)(b) measurable; reason: A is finite, so ∆(A) is the set of probability mass functions on A
--   - encoding: ∫ [gain from deviating] dµ ≤ ε → the gain is µ-integrable and its integral is ≤ ε; reason: in the paper's setting the gain is bounded and measurable, so integrability excludes nothing; it prevents the Bochner default 0 from making the test vacuous
--   - encoding: hyperfinite: generated by a Borel ℤ-action, or alternatively an increasing union of Borel finite equivalence relations (§2.3) → the increasing-union form; reason: the paper states the two forms as equivalent for CBERs (Dougherty–Jackson–Kechris 1994) and its proofs use the increasing-union form
--   - encoding: "a finite action set Aᵢ" → a finite non-empty action set; reason: a game needs every player to have an action; with an empty action set no strategy exists and the maximum in (4.1) is undefined
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Theorem 4.13, p.26; Definition 4.1, p.16

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Bayes_ckRel
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_Bayes_IsHarsanyiEq
import Definitions.Def_HellmanLevy_CBER_IsHyperfinite

namespace HellmanLevy.Bayes

/-- **Theorem 4.13 (main theorem).** Every hyperfinite Bayesian game with purely atomic types
admits a Harsányi ε-equilibrium, for every `ε > 0`: if the common knowledge relation of the
purely atomic Bayesian game `B` (finite non-empty player set, finite non-empty action sets that
may differ between players, a prior for each player, bounded Borel payoffs) is hyperfinite, then
there is a strategy profile `σ` with `∫ [maxₐ rⁱ(a, σ⁻ⁱ | tⁱ(ω)) − rⁱ(σ | tⁱ(ω))] dµⁱ(ω) ≤ ε` for
every player `i`.
Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), Theorem 4.13, p.26 (with Definition 4.1, p.16, "let ε > 0").

**Formalization Note** The state space is standard Borel (the paper's Polish space with its
Borel σ-algebra is one). Purely atomic types are built into `BayesianGame`. The proof's
reduction to a common action space is not a hypothesis: the action sets `A i` are arbitrary. -/
theorem theorem_4_13 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] [StandardBorelSpace Ω] {I : Type*}
    [Fintype I] [Nonempty I] [DecidableEq I] {A : I → Type*} [∀ i, Fintype (A i)]
    [∀ i, Nonempty (A i)] (B : BayesianGame Ω I A) (hB : CBER.IsHyperfinite (ckRel B))
    (ε : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) : ∃ σ : ∀ i, Ω → PMF (A i), IsHarsanyiEq B ε σ := by sorry

end HellmanLevy.Bayes
